+/*
+ * 0282: 0281 died on the first SMR read, so the SMMU is read-only from here.
+ *
+ * The global block of the apps SMMU reads fine and says a lot:
+ *
+ *   0281 SMMU in: sCR0 0x00200406 ID0 0x4c017e4c ID1 0x5000002d
+ *                 sGFSR 0x0 SYNR0 0x0 SYNR1 0x0
+ *
+ *   sCR0  CLIENTPD 0 (clients enabled), GFRE 1, GFIE 1, USFCFG 1
+ *         - fault reporting is on AND unassigned StreamIDs are made to fault,
+ *           so if the SMMU were refusing our DMA it would latch a global fault
+ *           with the SID in sGFSYNR1. That is a measurement, not a theory.
+ *   ID0   NUMSMRG 76, NUMSIDB 15 (mask 0x7fff, matches Linux), CTTW 1
+ *         (matches dmesg's "IDR0.CTTW overridden by dma-coherent"), S1TS 1,
+ *         S2TS 0 - this instance is stage-1 only, which is what
+ *         qcom,smmu-s1-bypass is about.
+ *   ID1   NUMCB 45 (matches dmesg's "45 context banks"), NUMS2CB 0.
+ *
+ * Those six numbers agreed with the Linux log line for line, so the block is
+ * clocked, the mapping is right and the reads are real. The next statement was
+ * the first SMR read at 0x15000800 - same 4 KiB page as sCR0 at 0x15000000 -
+ * and the ring ends there, mid-thread, with the machine still needing a hand to
+ * get back into recovery:
+ *
+ *   1141  SWEET USB: 0273 does not touch the QUSB2 PHY (0x88e2000) ...
+ *   1142  SWEET USB: 0281 SMMU in: sCR0 0x00200406 ...
+ *   <end of boot>
+ *
+ * 0280-d1 reached the same point and then printed the low-DMA window, so the
+ * wedge is the SMR access itself and not the moment. Until someone reads the
+ * downstream qsmmu-v500 driver, treat 0x15000800 like 0x088e2000: an address
+ * this kernel may not touch. The stream-matching tables are also very likely
+ * not where arm-smmu.h puts them - qcom,qsmmu-v500 is not a plain MMU-500, its
+ * TBUs have their own 4 KiB windows at 0x150c5000/0x150c9000/0x150cd000.
+ *
+ * So 0282 removes every write and every SMR access. What is left is the
+ * question 0281 could not answer: is the SMMU the thing that refuses the DMA?
+ * sGFSR.USF (bit 1) plus sGFSYNR1 == 0x740 after the prime says yes; a still
+ * zero sGFSR says the refusal comes from somewhere else and the two remaining
+ * suspects in section 38.3 (XPU/TZ, GDSC/clock) take over.
+ */
+
